  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Friday afternoon, broad northwesterly midlevel flow was \npresent across the central Plains with a shortwave trough sliding \nacross the ND/Canadian border. This shortwave was shunting a weak \ncold front extending from eastern ND through the OK panhandle. The \nprefrontal and post-frontal airmass is quite similar with \ntemperatures in the 50s to near 60 common. The weak cold front will \ncontinue to slide southeast into the evening hours before undergoing \nfrontolysis across south-central/southeast KS. Overnight lows will \nbe much warmer than the past several nights with lows in the low to \nmid 30s.\n\nLate Saturday into Saturday night, an amplifying midlevel shortwave \ntrough and inverted surface trough will slide across the region. \nThis will return rain chances to the area with the best chances \nacross central and eastern KS. Radar returns are likely to be \npresent by mid to late afternoon across central KS but a\nplethora of dry low-level air may delay the onset of rainfall \nto after 5-6 PM across central KS. These rain chances will \nspread southeastward throughout the evening and overnight. Total\nrain amounts are expected to remain generally less than 0.25\". \nA strong post-frontal pressure gradient will support gusty \nnorthwest winds. RRFS/RAP/HRRR forecast soundings suggest mean \nboundary layer winds behind the front will near 35 kt with wind \nspeeds at the top of the mixed layer around 40 kt. This supports\nwinds gusts up to 45 mph areawide Saturday night. Should these \ntrends hold, a Wind Advisory may be needed Saturday night. \n\nOtherwise, quasi-zonal midlevel flow will dominate the pattern for \nSunday into the middle of next week. High temperatures will remain \nmostly in the 50s. A modest pressure gradient will setup across the \nregion Monday as a lee surface trough deepens across the high \nPlains. This will set the stage for southerly wind gusts of 30-40 \nmph. Longer range guidance is suggesting a stronger midlevel\ntrough will approach the area for the second half of next week.",
